                        "entry_id": "756742e4-6ad2-3894-ac45-e4b9cf222b52",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The letter Ꝩ/ꝩ, used in Old Norse, related to the rune wynn (ᚹ, whence also Latin-script Ƿ/ƿ) but with the bowl open at the top, like a y."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "710d7b67-7797-4a3e-9cbf-148ec2950921",
                                "sense_id": "29f00aa3-a74c-3ace-8670-34cdc6425cf7",
                                "text": "[...] a gramm. term, implying the use of the old letter 'vend' in spelling v-rungu, v-rangr, v-reiðr, see introduction to letter R; ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6a3bf562-0e39-45b8-a3ab-26bfce173943",
                                "sense_id": "29f00aa3-a74c-3ace-8670-34cdc6425cf7",
                                "text": "In Old English, the meaning of wynn is the same. In Old Norse, the etymological equivalents of words beginning with w are spelled with a v, the letter named “vend” in the Icelandic alphabet."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
